Story: Complete the Exchange registration of the FIS OPF API and obtain the security approvals Points: 3

Description

The FIS OPF API (POM Customer Credit Transfer Initiation) is an external API consumed by the Wire II service, so it must be registered on Exchange as a third-party API and catalogued as a consumed dependency. The registration has been submitted from the FIS OpenAPI contract; this story carries it through the Cyber and ISO Assurance reviews, records the approved registration against the ASV that will host the Wire II service, and keeps the registered contract in step with the FIS specification version in use.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Third-party API registration for the FIS OPF API is approved on Exchange [ ] Cyber and ISO Assurance reviews are complete and their outcomes recorded on the Capability 2 page [ ] Registered contract matches the FIS specification version the Wire II service is built against [ ] Registration is linked to the hosting ASV and re-pointed when the Wire II ASV is ready [ ] Owner and renewal or re-review conditions for the registration are documented

Story: Establish FIS Code Connect connectivity to the OPF sandbox Points: 3

Description

Set up and prove the connection path from Capital One to the FIS OPF sandbox through FIS Code Connect: the Code Connect application and product subscription, OAuth 2.0 client-credentials token acquisition, and a first call to the customer-credit-transfer-initiations endpoint that reaches the OPF backend. Capture the exact token endpoint, scopes, subscription and headers that work, and the identifiers FIS must supply for the business application header, so the Wire II service can be configured from a known-good baseline.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Code Connect application and subscription to the OPF product exist for the sandbox [ ] OAuth 2.0 client-credentials token is obtained from the sandbox token endpoint [ ] A test request reaches the OPF sandbox backend and the response is captured, success or error [ ] Business application header identifiers and a known-good sample request are obtained from FIS, or the open request is tracked with a date [ ] Working configuration (endpoints, headers, subscription) is documented on the Capability 2 page

Story: Secure the OPF API connection: credentials, transport and data-protection exception Points: 5

Description

Put the OPF API credentials and transport protection on a supportable footing. Client id and secret for each environment are held in the approved secrets store, never in code or configuration files, with a documented rotation procedure agreed with FIS. Transport is TLS over Code Connect. Because the payload carries full account numbers and FIS has not offered payload-level encryption, the UTEP covering account numbers over TLS only is completed and approved; if FIS confirms a payload-encryption option on Code Connect, the decision to adopt it is recorded and a follow-up story created.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Client id and secret for sandbox and production are stored in the approved secrets store and read by the service at runtime [ ] Rotation procedure for the Code Connect secret is documented, including how often and who performs it [ ] TLS configuration and trusted certificate chain for Code Connect are documented and verified from the service runtime [ ] UTEP for account numbers over TLS only is submitted and approved under the hosting ASV [ ] FIS answer on payload-level encryption is recorded and the resulting decision noted on the Capability 2 page

Story: Open the network path from the hosting ASV to FIS Code Connect Points: 5

Description

Make the OPF API reachable from where the Wire II service runs. Identify the Code Connect endpoints and IP ranges for sandbox and production, raise the security-group egress and firewall rules from the hosting ASV (PIE now, the Wire II ASV when ready), settle whether the traffic must be NAT'd and whether FIS needs Capital One ranges allow-listed on its side, and verify the path end to end from the service runtime rather than from a laptop. The temporary firewall rules are covered by a UTEP if they are not baselined.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Code Connect endpoints and IP ranges for sandbox and production are confirmed with FIS [ ] Egress security-group and firewall rules are approved and applied for the hosting ASV [ ] NAT and FIS-side allow-list questions are answered and recorded [ ] Connectivity is verified from the service runtime in the non-production environment [ ] Temporary rules are either baselined or covered by an approved UTEP

Story: Obtain production OPF API credentials and configure the connection per environment Points: 3

Description

Complete the FIS onboarding for production and wire the connection settings into each environment. This covers the production Code Connect client credentials and organisation identifier from FIS, the per-environment configuration of base URLs, token endpoints and header values, and a production connectivity check that proves authentication and routing without initiating a payment. A short runbook records how to verify, rotate and troubleshoot the connection.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] Production client credentials and organisation identifier are received from FIS and stored in the secrets store [ ] Base URL, token endpoint and header values are configured for each environment and selected by environment, not by code change [ ] Production authentication and routing are verified with a non-payment call agreed with FIS [ ] Runbook covers verification, credential rotation, and the first checks when the OPF API is unreachable [ ] FIS contacts and escalation path for connection issues are recorded

Story: Build the shared OPF API client configuration for the Wire II service Points: 3

Description

Provide the Wire II service with one configured client for the OPF API so the connection established in this capability is used the same way everywhere: token acquisition with caching and refresh before expiry, the mandatory uuid and idempotency-key headers, connection and read timeouts suited to a synchronous call from Payment Exchange, and metrics and alerts for authentication failures, timeouts and 5xx responses. The client is generated from the registered FIS contract so the models stay in step with the specification.

Acceptance criteria

[ ] OAuth token is cached and refreshed before expiry; a failed refresh is retried and alerted [ ] uuid and idempotency-key headers are set on every call and traceable in logs without logging account data [ ] Connection and read timeouts are configurable per environment with agreed defaults [ ] Metrics and alerts exist for authentication failures, timeouts and 5xx responses [ ] Client models are generated from the registered FIS contract and rebuilt when the contract version changes
